1. Sequence Containers :-

i) List (implimented using doubly linked list):-
* Allow non-contiguous memory allocation
* slow traversal
* but once position is found, fast insertion, deletion

ii) Deque
* all functionalities of vectors plus features of push, pop at both the ends

iii) Array
* array class know its size, so we dont have to pass the size of array as a separate parameter to function
* at(), get(), fill(), size(), front(), back()

iv) Forward List(impliemnted using singly linked list) :-
* Advantage over List is because, it only stores the address of next element, not previous element, so no unneccessaary memory consumption
* Useful if only forward traversal is required.

2. Container Adaptiors :-

i) Queue
* FIFO arrangement
* inserted at the back and deleted from the front

ii) Priority Queue
* Create a max heap / mean heap automatically

iii) Stack
* LIFO workflow and do empty(), size(), top(), push(any), pop() in O(1)

3. Associative Containers :-

Impliment sorted DS that can be quizkly searched(O(log n))

i) Set
* Each element has to be unique
* value of elements can't be modified once added to set, but modified value can be added or deleted

ii) Multiset
* Similar to set with difference that, multiple element can have same value

iii) Map
* Stores object in key value pair and the key part is always sorted

iv) Multimap
* Similar to maps, plus some some other benefits of having repetative pair.

4. Unordered Associative Containers

Impliment unordered DS that can be quickly searched

i) unordered set
* implimented using a hash table, keys are hashed into indices of a hashed table
* insertion is always randomised
* practical operations take O(1) time and having worst case as O(n)

ii) unordered Multiset
iii) unordered map
iv) unordered Multimap


